@using Ben.Web.Services
@inject SiteFeaturesProvider Features

@*
    Wraps a page whose whole section a SuperAdmin can switch off.

    Renders its children when the feature is on, and the ordinary "page not found" body when it is
    not. Not a redirect and not an "unavailable" message, on purpose:

      • A redirect would still have answered the URL, and it tells the visitor that something is
        there but closed — which is more than a switched-off section should say.
      • A bespoke "this feature is disabled" screen leaks the site's configuration to anyone who
        guesses a path, and invites "when will it be back?" mail about a section that may never
        return.

    A section that is off should look exactly like a section that was never built, which is what
    NotFoundContent already says.

    This runs during the server render, so the URL genuinely stops working rather than the page
    drawing and then hiding itself. Hiding a navigation link while its address still answers is
    the failure this codebase keeps re-learning; the gate and the nav read the same provider so
    they cannot disagree.
*@

@if (_on)
{
    @ChildContent
}
else
{
    @*
        The same words as the site's NotFoundContent, deliberately duplicated: that component
        lives in Ben.Web.Website, which references this library and not the other way round, so
        it cannot be called from here. Two short paragraphs are a smaller price than inverting
        the project dependency — but they must stay in step, and a test asserts they do.
    *@
    <PageTitle>Page not found</PageTitle>
    <div class="text-center py-5">
        <h1 class="display-6">Page not found</h1>
        <p class="text-muted mb-4">There is nothing at this address.</p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/">Back to the home page</a>
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The feature key from <see cref="SiteFeatures"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Flag { get; set; } = default!;

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    private bool _on = true;

    // Read once per render pass rather than per use: the provider is cheap, but a page that
    // consults it from markup would ask again on every re-render for an answer that cannot
    // change mid-render.
    protected override void OnParametersSet() => _on = Features.IsOn(Flag);
}
